📌 PROJECT OVERVIEW

RetailMart Country Sales Analysis is a data analytics and business intelligence project designed to analyze retail sales and transaction data across multiple countries.

The project demonstrates an end-to-end analytics workflow, starting with source data preparation and continuing through database management, SQL analysis, calculated fields, and interactive dashboard development using Microsoft Power BI.

The project uses sales datasets associated with six countries:

- 🇨🇦 Canada
- 🇨🇳 China
- 🇮🇳 India
- 🇳🇬 Nigeria
- 🇬🇧 United Kingdom
- 🇺🇸 United States

The datasets contain transaction-level information, including transaction identifiers, dates, product details, prices, quantities, discounts, payment methods, customer demographic categories, store locations, and sales representatives.

PostgreSQL is used to store, inspect, validate, and analyze the sales data. SQL queries support data-quality checks and sales analysis, while Power BI provides interactive reports for exploring sales performance and transaction patterns.

The final objective is to transform raw sales records into a structured and understandable reporting solution that supports data-driven analysis.

Project Highlights

- Analysis of sales data from six countries.
- Source-data inspection and preparation.
- PostgreSQL database operations.
- SQL-based data-quality checks.
- Identification of null values and duplicate transaction identifiers.
- Calculation of total sales amounts and profit fields.
- Sales analysis by country, product, store location, and sales representative.
- Power BI KPI cards and interactive visualizations.
- Monthly, weekly, product, payment, customer-demographic, and geographic analysis where supported by the available data.
- Dashboard navigation and consistent visual formatting.

---

🎯 BUSINESS PROBLEM STATEMENT

Retail sales datasets contain large numbers of transaction records. Examining individual records manually makes it difficult to understand overall sales performance, compare products, identify important payment methods, and analyze changes over time.

Without a structured analytical process, it can also be difficult to identify missing values, duplicate transaction identifiers, inconsistent data, or incorrect calculated values.

This project addresses these challenges by organizing sales data in PostgreSQL, applying SQL-based analysis and validation, and presenting the results through Power BI dashboards.

The reporting solution is intended to make important sales indicators and transaction patterns easier to review and compare.

